    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">Here&#8217;s what I keep hearing from payment operations teams: <strong style="font-weight:700;">&#8220;We&#8217;re ISO 20022 compliant.&#8221;</strong></p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">And technically, they&#8217;re right. Their messages validate against the schema. Their XML passes format checks. Their middleware translates the old message format to the new one without errors. By every measurable standard of compliance, they&#8217;ve crossed the line.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">But when I ask about their address data quality — whether &#8220;London&#8221; in their payment messages means London, England or London, Ontario — the room goes quiet. When I ask how their screening engine handles a street name that happens to match a sanctioned country — blank stares.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 20px 0;">That silence is the sound of the last mile problem.</p>

    <!-- Callout: The Distinction -->
    <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 20px 0;">
    <tr>
      <td style="background:#F5F9FF;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-left:3px solid #43ADC1;border-radius:4px;padding:20px 24px;">
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 10px 0;">The Distinction That Changes Everything</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#466587;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;color:#232F3E;">Format compliance</strong> means your messages use ISO 20022 XML syntax correctly. Every address element validates. Your translator works.</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#466587;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;color:#232F3E;">Data readiness</strong> means the information <em>inside</em> those messages is structured, unambiguous, and actionable — that the city field contains a disambiguated city, that the country code is verified, that the entire address resolves deterministically across 195 countries and 50+ writing systems.</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#466587;margin:0;">The gap between them is where payment exceptions, sanctions screening false positives, and straight-through processing failures live.</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 20px 0;">We&#8217;ve spent the past quarter analysing this gap in detail. The terrain between format compliance and data readiness — and a practical framework for closing it before November 2026 — is what this issue is about.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;margin:0;color:#444444;">
      <strong style="font-weight:700;">Go deeper:</strong>
